  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Hot conditions this week.\n\nConfidence is increasing for a particularly warm week ahead of us, \nespecially on Wednesday and Thursday. A mid-level ridge from the \nwest will steadily make its way over to the Mid-Altantic by \nThursday. This higher pressure will keep precipitation chances down \nbut temperatures unseasonably high. High temperatures will gradually \nincrease each day and reach their peak by Thursday when the\ntemperatures may get to the lower 100s. With dew points \npersisting from the mid-60s to lower 70s all week, the heat \nindex values for the VA and NC Piedmont may approach 105-108 \ndegrees. The risk of 105 heat index values for Wednesday exists \nas well and Heat Advisories may be needed as a result for this \npart of the forecast area. For everyone else, aside from the \nhigher elevations, heat index values between 90-100F are\npossible. For safety reasons, the public is encouraged to stay \nhydrated and cool this week regardless of where you are at to \navoid heat related illnesses. It may be September, but it\ncertainly will not feel like it. A back door cold front may \nmove through this weekend and bring us back down to highs in the\nmid-70s to mid-80s by early next week.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Return of showers and thunderstorms possible this \nweekend.\n\nThe mid-level ridge over the Atlantic will lose some momentum \nand stop moving eastward by Thursday. There looks to be another\nchance for areawide showers and thunderstorms this weekend but \nnarrowing down the timing and severity is a bit of a challenge \nthis far out. On Friday, a cold front is set to move southwest \nand may pass through between 12Z Friday and 12Z Saturday. The \ntiming of the front is critical as more northwesterly mean flow \nwould follow it and move further south into the Mid-Atlantic,\npushing the ridge away. Severe weather could develop if the \ntiming of the front intersects the warmest and most unstable \npart of the day on Friday. Warm and moist surface conditions \nwill lead to surface level CAPE values of at least 1000-2000 \nJ/kg. The stronger northwesterly flow will lead to better lapse \nrates (up to 6.5 C/km) and the delivery of better wind shear \n(25-35 knots) and mid-level vorticity. While the better dynamics\nfor severe weather are projected to be to our northeast, the \nStorm Prediction Center does have parts of the Virginia Piedmont\nto the Alleghany Highlands highlighted to reflect the severe \nrisk. To reiterate, much of this relies on a lot of atmospheric \nconditions coming together at the right time.\n\nEven if Friday's storm set up does not materialize, the \nnorthwesterly flow would still send perturbations into the weekend \nand provide rainfall chances for Saturday and Sunday. By early next \nweek, a high surface pressure system begins to wedge itself into our \nmountains and offer a break in precipitation.",
